        content: "<div class=\"blog-answer-box\"><p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Open the transfer in the provider's app and compare its status with the delivery date on your receipt. Before that date, the causes providers list are identity or source-of-funds checks, weekends and holidays, and the recipient's bank. After it, ask the provider to trace the payment and, for SWIFT, give the recipient the UETR. US senders then have 180 days to report it as an error.</p></div>\n<p>Start with the delivery date on your receipt. A US provider must print the date the money will be available in the recipient's country, labelled \"Date Available\", and the CFPB's official interpretation says it <a href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1005/31/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cannot give a range of dates or an estimate</a>. UK and EU payment rules do not require a stated delivery time for transfers to countries outside them, so there you work from the estimate your provider showed you. Before that date passes, a slow transfer is something to wait out or answer. After it passes, it is something to report.</p>\n<p>Our own test transfers show the range. A TapTap Send payment to a German bank account was marked successful {{SPEED_TEST_TIME:t02}} after it was submitted. A Wise payment to a euro bank account came back to Wise in the same minute it was sent, went out again {{SPEED_TEST_RETRY:t06}} later, and was marked sent to the bank {{SPEED_TEST_TIME:t06}} after we set it up. These times come from each provider's own status timeline and stop at its last step. They do not show when the recipient's bank credited the money.</p>",
